      "answer_segment": "To determine how many more oranges Ana will have peeled than Jane after an hour, we need to calculate the number of oranges each of them can peel in that time and then find the difference.\n\nFirst, let's determine how many minutes each person can spend peeling oranges in an hour. Since there are 60 minutes in an hour, we can calculate the number of minutes each person can spend peeling oranges as follows:\n\nFor Ana:\n\\[ \\text{Minutes Ana can spend peeling} = \\frac{60}{3} = 20 \\text{ minutes} \\]\n\nFor Jane:\n\\[ \\text{Minutes Jane can spend peeling} = \\frac{60}{4} = 15 \\text{ minutes} \\]\n\nNext, we need to find out how many oranges each person can peel in those minutes. Since each orange takes 3 minutes to peel for Ana and 4 minutes to peel for Jane, we can calculate the number of oranges each person can peel as follows:\n\nFor Ana:\n\\[ \\text{Number of oranges Ana can peel} = \\frac{20}{3} \\approx 6.67 \\]\nSince the number of oranges must be a whole number, we take the integer part, so Ana can peel 6 oranges.\n\nFor Jane:\n\\[ \\text{Number of oranges Jane can peel} = \\frac{15}{4} = 3.75 \\]\nSince the number of oranges must be a whole number, we take the integer part, so Jane can peel 3 oranges.\n\nFinally, we find the difference in the number of oranges peeled by Ana and Jane:\n\\[ \\text{Difference} = 6 - 3 = 3 \\]\n\nTherefore, after an hour, Ana will have peeled \\(\\boxed{3}\\) more oranges than Jane.",
